#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
#
# Piece K — QUIC-specific block on the FORWARD path.
# Nemesis static-policy table. Window 1, 2026-08-06.
#
# ── WHY A SEPARATE TABLE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This is deliberately NOT part of `nemesis_enforce` (ADR 0019). That table has a
# single-authority constraint: it is populated exclusively by the enforcement
# engine from its own derived state, and anything else writing into it breaks the
# guarantee that its contents are attributable to that engine. Static policy is a
# different concern with a different lifecycle, so it gets its own table.
# It is also NOT a ufw rule: ufw is the access-control chokepoint
# (alert_manager/firewall.py), and a protocol fingerprint is not access control.
#
# ── PRIORITY, and why this one ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Measured on the gateway 2026-08-06, forward hook occupancy:
#     nemesis_enforce   priority raw    (-300)
#     ufw (ip/ip6 filter) + nemesis_gw_nat   priority filter (0)
# This table sits at `mangle` (-150): AFTER nemesis_enforce, so that engine keeps
# first say and its single authority is untouched; BEFORE ufw, so the QUIC
# decision is decisive rather than depending on ufw's accept rules further down.
# Do not move it to raw/-300 — that collides with the enforcement engine.
#
# ── THE MATCH, verbatim from the validated research ──────────────────────────
# Two fields in the clear at the front of every QUIC handshake packet:
#     byte 0 bit 7  Header Form = 1 (long header)
#     byte 0 bit 6  Fixed Bit   = 1
#     bytes 1-4     Version     = 0x00000001 (v1) or 0x6b3343cf (v2)
# @th,64,8  = first byte of the UDP payload (UDP header is 64 bits)
# @th,72,32 = the 4-byte version field
#
# Measured discrimination, 0 false positives across 24 packets:
#   real QUIC (aioquic -> Cloudflare + Google)   16 UDP:443 -> 11 matched
#   WireGuard, STUN, DTLS, RTP, game binary, DNS 18 UDP:443 ->  0 matched
#   adversarial near-miss (0xc0 first byte, bogus version)  3 ->  0 matched
# THE NEAR-MISS ROW IS WHY THE VERSION FIELD IS IN THE MATCH. Header-form
# matching alone gave 3/3 false positives. Do not "simplify" this rule.
#
# ── reject with icmpx, NOT icmp ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# `reject with icmp ...` makes nft silently add `meta nfproto ipv4`, which
# narrows the rule to IPv4. IPv6 QUIC then passes freely while the counter reads
# 0 — indistinguishable from "the mechanism does not work". Measured 2026-08-04.
# `icmpx` emits the right unreachable for whichever family matched.
#
# Reject rather than drop, deliberately: a client that gets an ICMP
# port-unreachable falls back to TCP/443 immediately. A silent drop makes it wait
# out a timeout first, which is what makes QUIC blocking feel like a broken
# network instead of a fast fallback.
#
# ── KNOWN LIMITS, carried forward, not resolved here ─────────────────────────
#   * QUIC v2 (0x6b3343cf) is in the match set but was NEVER observed on the
#     wire — that set entry is unverified by real traffic.
#   * Only Chrome 151 was measured. Firefox and Safari fallback are believed the
#     same and are NOT measured. Firefox is now testable via this gateway.
#   * Short-header (post-handshake) QUIC is not matched, by design: blocking the
#     handshake is sufficient and cheaper than tracking established flows.
#   * No throughput/CPU cost measured for this rule in the forward path.

# ── ATOMIC REPLACE — this preamble is load-bearing, do not delete it ─────────
# `nft -f` is ADDITIVE, not declarative: re-loading a file that merely defines a
# table APPENDS its rules to the existing one. Measured 2026-08-06 — three loads
# produced three identical reject rules. Since the systemd unit runs `nft -f` on
# every start, and the installer may run the deploy more than once, that stacks
# duplicates silently and makes the shared named counter over-count.
#
# The two lines below are the standard idiom: create the table if absent (so the
# delete cannot fail on a fresh boot), then delete it, then define it fresh. The
# whole file is applied as ONE transaction, so there is no window in which the
# block is missing.
#
# SIDE EFFECT, stated because it looks like data loss otherwise: the named
# counter RESETS on every reload. It counts since the last load, not since boot.
table inet nemesis_policy { }
delete table inet nemesis_policy

table inet nemesis_policy {
    counter quic_forward_blocked { }

    chain forward {
        type filter hook forward priority mangle; policy accept;

        udp dport 443 @th,64,8 & 0xc0 == 0xc0 @th,72,32 { 0x00000001, 0x6b3343cf } \
            counter name "quic_forward_blocked" \
            reject with icmpx type port-unreachable
    }
}
